```jsx
// One job: describe a single ticket row.
function TicketRow({ subject, assignee, status }) {
  return (
    <li className="ticket-row">
      <span className="subject">{subject}</span>
      <span className="assignee">{assignee}</span>
      <StatusBadge status={status} />
    </li>
  );
}

// One job: describe the list, and give every row a stable identity.
function TicketList({ tickets }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {tickets.map((ticket) => (
        <TicketRow
          key={ticket.id}
          subject={ticket.subject}
          assignee={ticket.assignee ?? 'Unassigned'}
          status={ticket.status}
        />
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

Notice what `TicketRow` does *not* do. It does not fetch, it does not sort, and it does not decide what `Unassigned` means — the parent settles that before it hands the value down. `TicketRow` can therefore be rendered in a style guide with three hard-coded props and it will look exactly as it does in production.

Also notice `key={ticket.id}`. It is set on the element produced *inside* `map`, not on the JSX inside `TicketRow`. React reads the key from the element it is placing into the list.

solution.code:
function toTicketRows(tickets) {
  return tickets.map(function (ticket) {
    const named = typeof ticket.assignee === 'string' && ticket.assignee.trim() !== '';
    return {
      key: ticket.id,
      title: ticket.subject,
      assignee: named ? ticket.assignee : 'Unassigned',
    };
  });
}

